@rem Exercises for lesson 03_memory (C++ track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise cpp-mem-theory-delete-array
@type theory
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which statement frees an array that was created with  int* arr = new int[10];  ? Write it for the variable arr.
@answer
delete[] arr; | delete [] arr; | delete[] arr | delete [] arr
@explain
Memory from new[] must be released with delete[]; plain delete would be undefined behavior.

@exercise cpp-mem-theory-heap-operator
@type theory
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which operator allocates memory on the heap and returns a pointer to it?
@answer
new | the new operator | new operator | operator new
@explain
new allocates on the heap; the memory stays until you delete it.

@exercise cpp-mem-choice-pair
@type choice
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which pairs of operators are correct?
@choices
- new with delete[]
- new[] with delete
* new with delete, and new[] with delete[]
- new with free
@explain
Every new needs exactly one delete, and every new[] needs exactly one delete[]. Mixing them is undefined behavior.

@exercise cpp-mem-choice-dangling
@type choice
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Which function returns a dangling pointer?
@choices
- int* f() { int* x = new int; *x = 5; return x; }
* int* f() { int x = 5; return &x; }
- int* f() { static int x = 5; return &x; }
- int* f() { return nullptr; }
@explain
The local x is destroyed when the function returns, so the returned address points to memory that no longer exists.

@exercise cpp-mem-predict-new
@type predict
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int* a = new int(5);
int* b = new int{6};
int* c = new int[3]{1, 2, 3};
cout << *a << " " << *b << " " << c[0] << c[1] << c[2] << endl;
delete a;
delete b;
delete[] c;
@answer
5 6 123
@explain
new int(5) and new int{6} create single ints with those values; new int[3]{1, 2, 3} creates an array.

@exercise cpp-mem-predict-funcptr
@type predict
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int Add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

int main() {
    int (*fp)(int, int) = Add;
    cout << fp(20, 10) << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
30
@explain
fp points to Add, so fp(20, 10) calls Add(20, 10).

@exercise cpp-mem-predict-global
@type predict
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int g = 10;

void change() {
    int g = 99;
    g = 100;
}

int main() {
    change();
    cout << g << endl;
    return 0;
}
@answer
10
@explain
The local g inside change hides the global g, so the global stays 10.

@exercise cpp-mem-fill-new-int
@type fill
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Write a function that returns a pointer to memory holding 5. The memory must outlive the function.
@code
int* getPtrToNewInt() {
    int* x = new ___;
    *x = 5;
    return x;
}
@answer
int | int(5) | int{5}
@explain
Memory from new lives on the heap and survives after the function returns (the caller must delete it).

@exercise cpp-mem-fill-array
@type fill
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Allocate an array whose size is decided at run time, then free it.
@code
int numItems = 4;
int* arr = new int[___];
for (int i = 0; i < numItems; ++i) {
    arr[i] = i * i;
}
delete ___ arr;
@answer
numItems
[]
@match loose
@explain
new int[numItems] creates the array; delete [] arr (delete[] arr) frees it.

@exercise cpp-mem-fill-2d
@type fill
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 3
@prompt
Complete the allocation of a rows x cols array of ints.
@code
int rows = 3;
int cols = 4;
int** arr2D = new int*[rows];
for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
    arr2D[row] = new int[___];
}
for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
    delete[] ___;
}
delete[] arr2D;
@answer
cols
arr2D[row]
@explain
Every row is its own array of cols ints. Free the rows first, then the array of row pointers.

@exercise cpp-mem-bug-mixed
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int* a = new int[3]{1, 2, 3};
    cout << a[0] << endl;
    delete a;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@answer
delete[] a;
@explain
Memory allocated with new[] must be freed with delete[].

@exercise cpp-mem-bug-stack-delete
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Click the line that is a bug.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    int* xPtr = &x;
    delete xPtr;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@explain
x lives on the stack and frees itself. delete is only allowed for memory that was created with new.

@exercise cpp-mem-bug-use-after-delete
@type bug
@lesson 03_memory
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Click the line that uses memory it must not use any more.
@code
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int* x = new int(5);
    delete x;
    cout << *x << endl;
    return 0;
}
@bugline 7
@explain
After delete the memory no longer belongs to the program. Print first, delete afterwards.
